---
import GuideLayout from '../../layouts/GuideLayout.astro';

const title = 'Next.js 16 Upgrade Guide: Every Breaking Change and How to Find It in Your Code';
const description =
  'Upgrading from Next.js 15 to 16: Node.js 20.9, Turbopack by default, fully async request APIs, middleware renamed to proxy, next/image defaults, removed config and next lint. With a search for each change.';
const path = '/guides/nextjs-15-to-16-upgrade-guide/';
const sources = [
  { title: 'Next.js: How to upgrade to version 16 (docs bundled with next 16.4.6)', url: 'https://nextjs.org/docs/app/guides/upgrading/version-16' },
  { title: 'Next.js: Codemods', url: 'https://nextjs.org/docs/app/guides/upgrading/codemods' },
  { title: 'Next.js: Upgrade with a coding agent', url: 'https://nextjs.org/docs/app/guides/upgrading/agent-upgrade' },
];
---

<GuideLayout {title} {description} canonicalPath={path} published="2026-11-23" modified="2026-11-24" {sources}>
  <Fragment slot="toc">
    <a class="block hover:text-violet-200" href="#before-you-start">Before you start</a>
    <a class="block hover:text-violet-200" href="#runtime">Node.js and TypeScript</a>
    <a class="block hover:text-violet-200" href="#turbopack">Turbopack by default</a>
    <a class="block hover:text-violet-200" href="#async-apis">Async request APIs</a>
    <a class="block hover:text-violet-200" href="#proxy">middleware becomes proxy</a>
    <a class="block hover:text-violet-200" href="#images">next/image defaults</a>
    <a class="block hover:text-violet-200" href="#caching">Caching APIs</a>
    <a class="block hover:text-violet-200" href="#routes">Routes and build output</a>
    <a class="block hover:text-violet-200" href="#removals">Removed APIs and config</a>
    <a class="block hover:text-violet-200" href="#checklist">Checklist</a>
  </Fragment>

  <p>Next.js 16 finishes several changes that Next.js 15 started. Some fail loudly at build time, like a custom <code>webpack</code> config or a parallel route without <code>default.js</code>. Others are quiet: synchronous <code>cookies()</code> calls, image quality coerced to 75, a Docker image still on Node.js 18. This guide lists every breaking change in the official upgrade guide, explains it in a sentence or two, and gives a quick search to find it in your repository.</p>
  <p>The searches use <a href="https://github.com/BurntSushi/ripgrep">ripgrep</a> (<code>rg</code>). They are deliberately simple: they can match code that is fine and miss code that isn't, so treat each result as a place to look, not a verdict.</p>

  <h2 id="before-you-start">Before you start</h2>
  <p>The official guide now recommends letting a coding agent run the upgrade with <code>npx next@canary upgrade --agent=latest</code>, which prepares the migration guides and the codemod for the agent and asks it to verify the app. The manual path is the <code>upgrade</code> codemod:</p>
  <pre><code>npx @next/codemod@canary upgrade latest</code></pre>
  <p>Know what the codemod covers. It moves Turbopack config out of <code>experimental</code>, migrates <code>next lint</code> to the ESLint CLI, renames <code>middleware</code> to <code>proxy</code>, drops <code>unstable_</code> prefixes from stabilized APIs and removes <code>experimental_ppr</code> segment config. It does <strong>not</strong> migrate synchronous request APIs left over from the Next.js 15 compatibility period; that is a separate codemod (<code>next-async-request-api</code>). Either way, review the diff yourself: a codemod that finishes cleanly only means it ran.</p>

  <h2 id="runtime">Node.js and TypeScript requirements</h2>
  <p>Next.js 16 needs Node.js 20.9 or later (Node.js 18 is no longer supported) and TypeScript 5.1 or later. Supported browsers are Chrome, Edge and Firefox 111+ and Safari 16.4+.</p>
  <p>The version in your <code>package.json</code> is rarely the only one that matters. Check every place a Node.js version is declared, because production runs whichever one the deploy path picks:</p>
  <pre><code>cat .nvmrc .node-version 2&gt;/dev/null
rg -n '"node"' package.json
rg -n '^FROM\s+node' -g 'Dockerfile*'
rg -n 'node-version' .github/workflows</code></pre>

  <h2 id="turbopack">Turbopack is the default bundler</h2>
  <p><code>next dev</code> and <code>next build</code> now use Turbopack without any flag, so <code>--turbopack</code> and <code>--turbo</code> in your scripts can go. If the project has a custom <code>webpack</code> config, <code>next build</code> now <strong>fails</strong> on purpose. You can build with Turbopack and ignore the webpack config (<code>next build --turbopack</code>), migrate the config to Turbopack options, or keep webpack with <code>next build --webpack</code>. A webpack config you didn't write can come from a plugin.</p>
  <p>Two related changes: <code>experimental.turbopack</code> moves to a top-level <code>turbopack</code> option, and Sass imports from <code>node_modules</code> can't use the legacy <code>~</code> prefix under Turbopack.</p>
  <pre><code>rg -n 'webpack' -g 'next.config.*'
rg -n -- '--turbo(pack)?\b' package.json
rg -n "@import\s+['\"]~" -g '*.scss' -g '*.sass'</code></pre>

  <h2 id="async-apis">Request APIs are async only</h2>
  <p>Next.js 15 made these APIs asynchronous but kept temporary synchronous access. Next.js 16 removes that compatibility, so the following can only be awaited:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><code>cookies()</code>, <code>headers()</code> and <code>draftMode()</code></li>
    <li><code>params</code> in layouts, pages, route handlers, <code>default.js</code> and the image metadata files</li>
    <li><code>searchParams</code> in pages</li>
  </ul>
  <pre><code>// Next.js 16
import &#123; cookies &#125; from 'next/headers';

export default async function Page() &#123;
  const session = (await cookies()).get('session');
  // ...
&#125;</code></pre>
  <p>The image-generating functions in <code>opengraph-image</code>, <code>twitter-image</code>, <code>icon</code> and <code>apple-icon</code> now receive <code>params</code> and <code>id</code> as promises, and the <code>sitemap</code> function receives its <code>id</code> as a promise. <code>generateImageMetadata</code> still gets synchronous <code>params</code>. For types, <code>npx next typegen</code> generates <code>PageProps</code>, <code>LayoutProps</code> and <code>RouteContext</code> helpers.</p>
  <pre><code>rg -n '\b(cookies|headers|draftMode)\(\)\s*\.' app src
rg -n 'params\.\w+|searchParams\.\w+' app src
rg -n 'UnsafeUnwrapped' app src</code></pre>
  <p>The last search finds casts the async codemod leaves behind when it can't migrate a call safely; each one is unfinished work.</p>

  <h2 id="proxy"><code>middleware</code> becomes <code>proxy</code></h2>
  <p>The <code>middleware</code> file name and the <code>middleware</code> named export are deprecated in favor of <code>proxy</code> (<code>proxy.ts</code>, <code>export function proxy</code>). Config flags follow, for example <code>skipMiddlewareUrlNormalize</code> becomes <code>skipProxyUrlNormalize</code>.</p>
  <p>One catch matters more than the rename: <code>proxy</code> always runs on the Node.js runtime and can't use <code>edge</code>. If your middleware relies on the edge runtime, keep it as <code>middleware</code> for now rather than renaming it blindly.</p>
  <pre><code>ls middleware.* src/middleware.* 2&gt;/dev/null
rg -n "runtime\s*[:=]\s*['\"]edge" -g 'middleware.*'
rg -n 'skipMiddlewareUrlNormalize' -g 'next.config.*'</code></pre>

  <h2 id="images"><code>next/image</code> defaults and restrictions</h2>
  <p>Six changes, most of them silent:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Query strings on local images</strong> now need <code>images.localPatterns.search</code>, to prevent enumeration attacks.</li>
    <li><strong><code>minimumCacheTTL</code></strong> goes from 60 seconds to 4 hours. Images whose source doesn't send <code>cache-control</code> refresh far less often.</li>
    <li><strong><code>imageSizes</code></strong> no longer includes 16.</li>
    <li><strong><code>qualities</code></strong> defaults to <code>[75]</code>. A <code>quality</code> prop outside the list is coerced to the nearest allowed value, and direct <code>/_next/image</code> requests with an unsupported <code>q</code> return 400.</li>
    <li><strong>Local IP optimization</strong> is blocked by default (<code>images.dangerouslyAllowLocalIP</code> opts back in; understand the SSRF risk first).</li>
    <li><strong><code>maximumRedirects</code></strong> goes from unlimited to 3.</li>
  </ul>
  <p><code>next/legacy/image</code> and <code>images.domains</code> are deprecated; use <code>next/image</code> and <code>images.remotePatterns</code>.</p>
  <pre><code>rg -n 'quality=' app src components
rg -n "src=['\"]/[^'\"]*\?" app src components
rg -n 'minimumCacheTTL|qualities|imageSizes|domains' -g 'next.config.*'
rg -n 'next/legacy/image' app src components</code></pre>

  <h2 id="caching">Caching APIs</h2>
  <p><code>revalidateTag</code> now takes a second argument, a <code>cacheLife</code> profile such as <code>'max'</code>; the one-argument form is deprecated and fails type-checking. For read-your-writes updates in Server Actions there is a new <code>updateTag</code>, and <code>refresh</code> refreshes the client router from a Server Action. <code>cacheLife</code> and <code>cacheTag</code> are stable, so their <code>unstable_</code> import aliases can go.</p>
  <pre><code>rg -n "revalidateTag\(\s*['\"][^'\"]+['\"]\s*\)" app src
rg -n 'unstable_(cacheLife|cacheTag)' app src</code></pre>

  <h2 id="routes">Routes, scrolling and build output</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Parallel routes</strong>: every slot (<code>@folder</code>) needs an explicit <code>default.js</code>, or the build fails. One that calls <code>notFound()</code> or returns <code>null</code> keeps the old behavior.</li>
    <li><strong>Scroll behavior</strong>: Next.js no longer overrides a global <code>scroll-behavior: smooth</code> during navigation. Add <code>data-scroll-behavior="smooth"</code> to <code>&lt;html&gt;</code> to keep the old override.</li>
    <li><strong>Dev and build output</strong>: <code>next dev</code> writes to <code>.next/dev</code> and a lockfile stops two dev or build processes on one project.</li>
    <li><strong>Build output</strong>: the <code>size</code> and <code>First Load JS</code> columns are gone from <code>next build</code>. Measure with Lighthouse or field Core Web Vitals instead.</li>
    <li><strong>Config loading</strong>: in <code>next dev</code>, <code>process.argv</code> no longer includes <code>'dev'</code> inside your Next config; use <code>NODE_ENV</code> or the config <code>phase</code>.</li>
  </ul>
  <pre><code>find app -type d -name '@*' -exec sh -c 'ls "$1"/default.* &gt;/dev/null 2&gt;&amp;1 || echo "missing default: $1"' _ &#123;&#125; \;
rg -n "process\.argv" -g 'next.config.*'</code></pre>

  <h2 id="removals">Removed APIs and config</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>AMP</strong>: all AMP APIs and config are gone (<code>amp</code> config, <code>next/amp</code>, <code>useAmp</code>, <code>config = &#123; amp: true &#125;</code>).</li>
    <li><strong><code>next lint</code></strong> is removed and <code>next build</code> no longer lints. Run ESLint or Biome directly; the <code>eslint</code> option in the Next config is removed too. <code>@next/eslint-plugin-next</code> now defaults to ESLint flat config.</li>
    <li><strong>Runtime config</strong>: <code>serverRuntimeConfig</code> and <code>publicRuntimeConfig</code> are removed. Use environment variables, with <code>NEXT_PUBLIC_</code> for values the browser needs.</li>
    <li><strong><code>devIndicators</code></strong> loses <code>appIsrStatus</code>, <code>buildActivity</code> and <code>buildActivityPosition</code>.</li>
    <li><strong>PPR and Cache Components</strong>: the experimental PPR flag and <code>experimental_ppr</code> segment config are removed, as are <code>experimental.dynamicIO</code> and <code>experimental.useCache</code>. PPR now comes through <code>cacheComponents</code>, which is a new model rather than a rename: enabling it can surface build errors for uncached data outside <code>&lt;Suspense&gt;</code>. If you weren't using these flags, delete them.</li>
    <li><strong><code>unstable_rootParams</code></strong> is removed in favor of <code>next/root-params</code>.</li>
    <li><strong>Later 16.x minors</strong> remove more flags: <code>experimental.viewTransition</code> (16.3) and <code>experimental.appNewScrollHandler</code> (16.4).</li>
  </ul>
  <pre><code>rg -n 'next lint' package.json .github
rg -n "next/amp|useAmp|amp:\s*true" app pages src
rg -n 'serverRuntimeConfig|publicRuntimeConfig|dynamicIO|useCache|ppr|devIndicators|viewTransition|appNewScrollHandler' -g 'next.config.*'
rg -n 'experimental_ppr|unstable_rootParams' app src</code></pre>

  <h2 id="checklist">Checklist before you merge</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Every Node.js version source (version files, <code>engines</code>, Dockerfiles, CI, the host) is on 20.9 or later.</li>
    <li>Build scripts match the bundler you actually use, including plugins that add a webpack config.</li>
    <li>No synchronous <code>cookies()</code>, <code>headers()</code>, <code>draftMode()</code>, <code>params</code> or <code>searchParams</code> access remains, and no <code>UnsafeUnwrapped</code> casts.</li>
    <li>Middleware that needs the edge runtime was not renamed to <code>proxy</code>.</li>
    <li>Image requests still work under the new defaults, with the qualities you intend.</li>
    <li><code>revalidateTag</code> calls pass a <code>cacheLife</code> profile.</li>
    <li>Every parallel route slot has a <code>default.js</code>.</li>
    <li>Nothing calls <code>next lint</code>, AMP, runtime config or removed flags.</li>
    <li>The app's own checks pass and the affected routes behave the same. A green build alone doesn't prove that.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="neukee">Where Neukee fits</h2>
  <p>Neukee is an open-source, local-first scanner for these upgrades. Version 0.1.0, planned for December 8, 2026, is built to flag synchronous request APIs, middleware that must become proxy, custom webpack config, removed config keys, <code>next lint</code> scripts and parallel route slots without <code>default</code>, plus Node.js version drift across your repo, each linked to the official note it comes from. Follow it on <a href="https://github.com/neukee/neukee">GitHub</a>.</p>
</GuideLayout>
